          "type": "turn",
          "question": "possibly nothing but my son ate a peanut bar and now his mouth feels funny and hes making a whistling sound when he breathes in",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now (999 in the UK, 911 in the US, or your local emergency number). Do not wait to see whether symptoms pass.** A whistling sound when breathing in, plus a strange feeling in the mouth after eating peanut, can be a sign of a severe allergic reaction (anaphylaxis) that affects the airway. Tell them your son ate peanut and is having trouble breathing. If you have a prescribed adrenaline auto-injector for him, use it now as you were taught, then still call for help.\n\nThe risk tool returned a \"routine\" result, but it did not match this description to any emergency pattern. That result is not reassurance. Please treat this as an emergency. The reference documents I searched did not cover this situation, so this advice does not come from them.\n\n**Get help faster and watch closely for these signs, which mean the situation is getting worse:**\n- Breathing that is harder, faster, or noisier, or pulling in at the chest or neck\n- Drooling, or being unable to swallow saliva\n- A hoarse, muffled, or changed voice\n- Swelling of the lips, tongue, or face, or new hives\n- Pale or grey skin, or lips that look blue\n- Confusion, unusual drowsiness, or floppiness\n- Vomiting, or collapse\n\nIf he becomes floppy, stops responding normally, or stops breathing, start CPR if you know how and keep calling for help.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
